WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Decision trees.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:07.300
A tree is just a stack of if and else questions, so you can read exactly why it decided.

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.567
At every node, the tree picks a feature and a threshold.

4
00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:18.700
It chooses the split that makes the two child groups as pure as possible, measured by Gini impurity or entropy.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.867
Then it repeats inside each child.

6
00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:23.467
Here is a depth two tree.

7
00:00:23.767 --> 00:00:26.000
The first question is about temperature.

8
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.800
If it is cool enough, we ask a second question, about radius.

9
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.510
Prediction is just walking a path from the root to a leaf.

10
00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:35.333
Cool and small means candidate.

11
00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:37.667
But there is a catch.

12
00:00:37.967 --> 00:00:41.100
Grown to full depth, a tree memorizes the training set.

13
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.271
Training accuracy climbs toward perfect, while test accuracy falls behind.

14
00:00:46.271 --> 00:00:49.167
Depth limits and minimum leaf sizes tame it.

15
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:51.933
Here it is in scikit-learn.

16
00:00:52.233 --> 00:00:56.233
We limit the depth to three, and export the tree as plain text rules.

17
00:00:56.533 --> 00:01:00.668
Then we grow one with no limit, and compare train and test scores.

18
00:01:00.668 --> 00:01:01.733
Expect a big gap.

19
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:06.033
Here is why trees matter even when they are mediocre.

20
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:09.333
A domain scientist will not trust the model said so.

21
00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:17.700
So you hand them the printed depth three tree: temperature under three fifty kelvin, radius under one point eight Earths, means candidate.

22
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.720
They recognize a crude habitable zone rule.

23
00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.567
That trust lets the random forest replace it.

24
00:01:24.767 --> 00:01:25.512
Let's recap.

25
00:01:25.512 --> 00:01:27.500
A tree is recursive if and else.

26
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.633
Each split makes the groups purer.

27
00:01:29.933 --> 00:01:31.733
Prediction walks from root to leaf.

28
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:35.142
Deep trees memorize, and ensembles fix that.

29
00:01:35.142 --> 00:01:36.767
Next up: random forest.
